WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.650
SPEAKER: Here at
Google, we continue

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:04.470
to see a lot of developers
building their APIs on top

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:05.940
of microservices.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:08.250
So in this example, we've
deployed a weather API

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:09.420
on Cloud Run.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:12.300
It's made up of four
different services.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.280
This is great for you,
as an API provider,

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.079
because it gives you
a lot of flexibility

00:00:16.079 --> 00:00:19.510
around how you deploy and
how you scale these services.

00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:21.060
But as an API
consumer, you don't

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:22.530
want to keep track
of which service

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:25.620
is responsible for
which piece of the API.

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:27.120
You just want one
interface that you

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:29.610
can call to access all
the capabilities provided

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:31.450
by this API.

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:34.470
So let's take a look at how
Google Cloud's API Gateway can

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:37.290
help you combine all
these services into one

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:39.090
easy-to-use API.

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:41.700
To get started, we'll need
an open API specification

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:44.790
that defines each of the
resources in our API.

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:47.070
Next, we'll add
instructions to tell the API

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:49.050
Gateway where to route
the request for each

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:50.970
of those resources.

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:52.740
Finally, we'll want
to secure our API,

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:54.930
so we'll configure a
security definition

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:59.320
that requires all incoming calls
to provide a valid API key.

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:01.290
Once we have our open
API specification,

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:03.780
we're ready to create
our API gateway.

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:05.700
First, we'll specify
the display name

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:08.580
and give our API a
unique identifier.

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:11.400
Next, we'll define the
API's initial configuration

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:14.340
by uploading our open API file.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.060
We'll then give our
configuration a Display Name

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:18.920
and we'll configure
the Service Account

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:22.320
that will be used to call
our backend services.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.990
Finally, we're ready to
deploy our API configuration

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:26.580
to a new gateway.

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:30.340
In this case, that gateway will
run in the US Central region.

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:32.320
Once we hit Create,
after a few minutes,

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:35.320
our gateway will be ready to
start accepting incoming API

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:37.830
requests.

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:40.770
Because we've secured our API,
we'll need valid credentials.

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:43.620
In this case, we've elected to
use API keys as our security

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:45.240
mechanism.

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:48.870
We can get an API key for our
API in the APIs and Services

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:51.150
section of the
Google Cloud Console.

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:53.850
Once we have our key,
we can make an API call

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:56.190
from the command
line using curl.

00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:59.010
If we try to access our API
without providing a key,

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:01.440
the call will be
rejected by the Gateway.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.110
Once we add our
key to the request,

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:06.390
the Gateway then allows our
call to go through successfully.

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:07.890
That was just a
quick preview of how

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:12.110
you can use the new API Gateway
to create and secure your APIs.

